  "note": "e2e fixture for #870/#891/#900: the shipped ksp_system Kerbol and Kerbin copied VERBATIM -- radius, mass, seed, amplitude, atmosphere, rotation and orbit all as shipped -- with one building: a pad at lat_deg 90. Why an authored file now: the shipped polar_pad used to be authored at lat 90, which is how four cases reached a hull parked where |omega x r| -> 0 on a world that DOES turn (Kerbin's day is 6 h, so the equator moves ~175 m/s and the pole moves none). Kerbin's pole ground sits 328 m under Kerbin's own sea, so #1007 moved the shipped pad to the nearest dry flat ground (88.8/171.4) -- 240 km off the pole, where the ground moves ~3.6 m/s and the state these cases want is gone. The fixture keeps it: same body, same air, same rotation, so every band those cases measure carries over unchanged. The pad stands at the pole on the sea floor here on purpose -- has_sea and sea_level stay as shipped because seaLevelRadius() is what the altitude columns read, and no physics term reads the ocean (that is exactly why a submerged pad looked broken rather than playing broken). #1063 then deleted the shipped polar_pad outright: the CLI's site= and the VAB dropdown both pick a pad by name, so a shipped pad named for a pole it no longer stood on had no job left. This file is now the only place in the repo where a pad stands at a pole. The id stays -- the four cases ask for it by name -- and the POLAR_PAD flag goes with the shipped pad, because nothing in src/ ever read it. Pinned by e2e/cases/render/870-prg-dash-on-a-polar-pad.txt, flight/891-sas-prograde-with-no-conic-refuses.txt, flight/900-sas-normal-with-no-plane-refuses.txt and flight/900-ascent-row-dashes-an-inclination-without-a-plane.txt.",
